  "query_preview": "..Satellite shows scattered mid-level clouds with\nbroad high pressure dominating the Southwest. A weak shortwave\nwill be moving through Arizona today leading to an isolated\nstorm or two over the White Mountains. Some buildups may also be\npossible along the international border in Cochise County. \nOtherwise expect scattered cumulus development with temperatures\n3-10 degrees above normal. Thursday slight chances for precip\nremain over the White Mountains ands wind become easterly. \nTemperatures will stay 3-10 degrees above normal through Friday\nwith easterly breezes sticking around into Saturday. \n\nThis weekend the upper air pattern becomes more progressive as a\ntrough sets up over the West Coast. This will have implications\nfor Hurricane Rachel, currently a few hundred miles west of \nCabo. As the trough digs and expands south it is expected to \ncause Rachel to make a 90 degree turn and head northeast. The \ncurrent NHC forecast shows this with Rachel turning northeast \nearly Friday and becoming post-tropical by Saturday. There is \nstill a good amount of uncertainty with where the remnants of \nRachel will make landfall. Current ensemble tracks are as far \nnorth as Oxnard and as far south as northern Baja California \nSur. Even with the track uncertainty southeast Arizona, \nespecially areas west of Tucson, will see an increase in \nmoisture starting Saturday into early next week. On top of this \nanother tropical system may make its way into the southern Gulf \nCalifornia by Monday, helping to send even more moisture. \nAlthough right now ensembles keep the bulk of the moisture from\nthis second system more in New Mexico than Arizona. Regardless,\nthis moisture will interact with the trough leading to rain \nchances for most of southeast Arizona starting Saturday through \nat least Tuesday. Ensemble mean rainfall amounts during this \ntime are 0.25\"-0.75\" with localized areas seeing 1\"-1.5\". The \nbulk of this rainfall will likely fall Sunday into Monday. The \ntrough will also bring breezy southwest winds Sunday and Monday \nwith speeds 15-25 mph. Temperatures will cool to around normal \nSunday, then below normal Monday into the middle of next week.",
